module ssoossh_agent 1.1;

# SELinux policy for ssoossh-agent.service, the ssh-agent that holds
# ssoosshd's CA key (deploy/ssoossh-agent.service).
#
# The unit runs /usr/bin/ssh-agent, which the targeted policy labels
# ssh_agent_exec_t. The policy defines the agent's domain per user role only
# (staff_ssh_agent_t, sysadm_ssh_agent_t, user_ssh_agent_t), with no generic
# ssh_agent_t and no transition from init_t, so an agent systemd starts
# stays in init_t. init_t may only getattr and relabel that type, so the
# unit fails in one of two ways under enforcing:
#
#   - status=203/EXEC: execve() is refused (no execute, open or read).
#   - signal=SEGV: execve() got past its point of no return and the kernel
#     was then refused the mapping of the binary (no map; the conditional
#     map rule is behind domain_can_mmap_files, off by default). The kernel
#     can no longer return an error, so it kills the process with SIGSEGV:
#     no fault address, no "segfault at" line, and no core dump.
#
# Both measured on AlmaLinux 9.8 and 10.2, each with its AVC: a denied
# execute (logged against the faccessat2 systemd makes before it execs),
# then a denied map.
#
# This module grants exactly what that exec needs, and the agent keeps
# running in init_t. ssh-pkcs11-helper, which ssh-agent execs for
# `ssh-add -s`, carries the same ssh_agent_exec_t label, so this one rule
# covers a token behind the agent as well. A dedicated confined domain was
# left out on purpose: `ssh-add -s` makes the agent dlopen a vendor PKCS#11
# library, and a confined domain would have to anticipate every vendor's
# files, devices and sockets, which is why the unit's own sandboxing is
# deliberately light.
#
# Plain module syntax, not refpolicy macros, so it builds both through
# scripts/build-selinux.sh (the selinux-policy-devel Makefile) and by hand,
# and the two compile to the same policy:
#
#   checkmodule -M -m -o ssoossh_agent.mod ssoossh_agent.te
#   semodule_package -o ssoossh_agent.pp -m ssoossh_agent.mod
#   semodule -i ssoossh_agent.pp
#
# The version is 1.1, the version of the module first written for this
# (ssoossh issue #214), so the shipped module and one built by hand from
# that issue read the same. It is a label only: the policy store does not
# compare versions (a 1.0 installed over this 1.1 is accepted), and which
# of two copies is active is decided by priority, see agent-postinstall.sh.

require {
	type init_t;
	type ssh_agent_exec_t;
	class file { execute execute_no_trans open read getattr map };
}

allow init_t ssh_agent_exec_t:file { execute execute_no_trans open read getattr map };
